text:
Below is an example of a complete .gv file that I created on a coffee shop’s daily customer traffic. Your final systems maps will looks something like this. Use this example to follow along as you read through DOT Explainer
This file is not posted on Canvas. Download it from the class GitHub page instead: Coffee-Shop-Example-Graphviz.gv. Click the download button to download the file from the browser.
digraph CoffeeShopSystem {

  rankdir=TD;

  node [shape=box, style="rounded, filled", fillcolor="white", fontname="Arial"];

  edge [fontname="Arial", fontsize=10];

  // Team Number

  // Class Section

  // Team Member Names:

  // --- Nodes (5 elements) ---

  customers  [label="Daily customer count"];

  buzz       [label="Word-of-mouth / online buzz"];

  quality    [label="Coffee and service quality"];

  wait_time  [label="Average wait time in line"];

  staff      [label="Baristas scheduled per shift"];

  // --- Causal links (with polarity) ---

  customers -&gt; buzz     [label="+"];         // More customers visiting generates more word-of-mouth and online reviews.

  buzz -&gt; customers     [label="+ delay"];    // Positive buzz attracts new customers, though it takes time to spread and for people to act on it.

  quality -&gt; buzz       [label="+"];         // Consistently good coffee and service generates positive buzz on its own, independent of how many people are currently visiting.

  customers -&gt; wait_time [label="+"];        // More customers in the shop at once means longer lines.

  staff -&gt; wait_time    [label="-"];         // More baristas working a shift serve customers faster, shortening the line.

  wait_time -&gt; staff    [label="+ delay"];    // When wait times run long, the manager schedules more baristas for future shifts to compensate.

  // --- Loop callouts (subgraph clusters) ---

  subgraph cluster_R1 {

    label="R1 Reinforcing: word-of-mouth growth";
    style="rounded,dashed";
    color="gray40";
    fontname="Arial";
    fontsize=12;

    customers;
    buzz;

    /* More customers generate more buzz; more buzz attracts more customers. Both links are positive, so the effect compounds - the simplest possible reinforcing structure. */

  }

  subgraph cluster_B1 {

    label="B1 Balancing: staffing responds to wait times";
    style="rounded,dashed";
    color="gray40";
    fontname="Arial";
    fontsize=12;

    wait_time;
    staff;

    /* Long wait times prompt the manager to schedule more staff; more staff shortens the wait. One link positive, one negative - the loop corrects itself instead of compounding. */

  }

}
